			    Using the TENX program

     The TENX program is provided with the TEN handler to allow the user to
conveniently make changes once the handler is installed in the system.  The
changes desired are specified by an option list which follows the '.R TENX'
command.  Parameters which can be changed are: the device codes used by the
interface, the baud rate, the time-out constant and a switch that determines
whether or not multiple OS/8 files will be combined into one large '10 file.
The options available are:

		/Normal[=xx]		KL-8E,-JA, M707, etc. interfaces
		/Decstation[=2,3]	VT/WT/78,'278: SLU2,3 ('none'=3)
		/Input=xx		Changes only input device codes
		/Output=xx		Changes only output device codes
		/Combine		Omits CTRL/Z after output files
		/Timeout=nnnn		Sets input timeout constant
		/Speed=nnnn		Programs baud rate of Decstation
		/Baudrate=nnnn		Same as Speed option
		/Version		Prints version number of program

     Several options may be included on one command line and only the first
letter of the switch is significant (the order makes no difference).

     Example:			.R TENX/N=56/T=4

     This sets the input device code to 56, the output device code to 57 (a
'normal' interface has 'output=input+1') and sets the time constant to '4'.
The latter is an empirical parameter which determines the length of time be-
fore the handler concludes that the '10 has stopped sending data and closes
the input file; typical values are 0-7, depending upon the response time of
the system.

     To combine several OS/8 output files into one large '10 file, use the
'/C' option.  This inhibits sending EOF codes (Ctrl/Z) after each OS/8 file
and hence allows successive files to be concatenated.  Use the '/N' option
to return to normal operation (one OS/8 file per PDP10 file).

     The speed (baudrate) options apply only to use on a Decstation and are
provided so that the user may conveniently change this important parameter.
Note that this change is made only by the TENX program, not by the handler
itself.  The allowable baudrates are: 50, 75, 110, 134, 150, 300, 600, 1200
1800, 2000, 2400, 3600, 4800, 7200, 9600 and 19200.  The TERMIN program has
the same options, and will normally be used to set this parameter.

     The TENX program may be run under BATCH and hence may be used to change
the characteristics of the handler between job steps.  Any errors in the op-
tion specifications cause the program to abort after printing one of the fol-
lowing messages:

	TEN HANDLER NOT FOUND		The handler isn't available
	INCORRECT VERSION		You don't have Version 'D'
	INPUT DVC = OUTPUT DVC		Device code error
	BAD SPEED OR BAUD RATE		Check the list above
	SYSTEM READ ERROR		SERIOUS problem on system dev
	SYSTEM WRITE ERROR		Write-locked maybe?  Try again

Any problems encountered with this program should be reported to:

